KZ2 6084: LD_TEX_SKIP, the live draw's 16-unit texture resolution reused from the previous draw
when everything it reads is unchanged. An earlier change's counters (2158, RSX_PERF_TEXREG) found 56 % of heavy-view draws
with all 16 units' registers unchanged from the draw before (91 % of units unchanged); the loop decodes each unit,
scans the sampler cache, the colour surfaces and the zetas for it, then calls texture_srv_slot. The reuse rule is in
libs/video/rsx_tex_skip.h (new): the 16 x 9 raw words, the fragment program's unit mask, the colour-surface and zeta
generations (g_ld_surf_gen / g_ld_zgen, bumped where surface_get and the zeta creation set an address and size) and
the device are equal, and the recording draw resolved every bound unit on the plain texture path with no colour
surface and no zeta at any bound address. A reuse takes the decoded units, unit mask and sampler slots (the sampler
cache is append-only) from the memo and still calls texture_srv_slot for every bound unit, so content coherence
(write watch, per-frame hash, re-decode, LRU) is unchanged. LD_TEX_SKIP=1 reuses, LD_TEX_SKIP=verify runs the loop on
every draw and counts a memo answer that differs as bad; "[tex-skip]" every 600 frames. Default off; off whenever
LD_UNITS, LD_PS1_BUF0, LD_UVDBG, LD_ALIAS_DBG, the upload check (6032), the zeta probe, the alias-size log or the
a010 probe is on. Host test: tools/runtime/test_tex_skip.sh (the rule with a negative control, and the wiring).
Review fix: a draw whose run-time diagnostic gate is on (zeta probe gate, a010 probe, upload check,
alias-size log) drops the memo (ld_tsk_begin) and keeps no decoded units (g_ld_tsk_t is written only when the skip
is on for the draw), and verify also compares the memo's decoded units with the fresh decode (ld_tsk_units_same).

